1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,922
Two systems. The first wins 80% of its trades. The second only 45%.

2
00:00:12,372 --> 00:00:19,137
Most people would pick the first. But in money, the second earns three and a half times more.

3
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:23,951
Today we judge a system with a full report card, not one number.

4
00:00:24,951 --> 00:00:29,783
Hello, this is episode eight of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

5
00:00:30,232 --> 00:00:35,641
Last time: Monte Carlo. One backtest, a thousand paths.

6
00:00:36,091 --> 00:00:41,420
Today: the system report card. Profit factor and Sharpe ratio.

7
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:45,345
Profit factor is the simplest number.

8
00:00:45,795 --> 00:00:51,516
Add up the profit of all winning trades. Add up the loss of all losing trades.

9
00:00:51,966 --> 00:00:56,250
Divide the profit by the loss. That is the profit factor.

10
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:02,814
System Y: 100 trades. 45 wins, each win 2R.

11
00:01:03,264 --> 00:01:10,422
So profit is 90R. 55 losses of 1R each, so the loss is 55R.

12
00:01:10,872 --> 00:01:18,107
90 divided by 55: a profit factor of 1.64. Net gain 35R.

13
00:01:19,107 --> 00:01:27,232
Now System X: 80 wins out of 100, but each win only 0.5R. Profit 40R.

14
00:01:27,681 --> 00:01:33,089
20 losses, but each loss 1.5R. Loss 30R.

15
00:01:33,538 --> 00:01:41,350
A profit factor of 1.33 and a net gain of only 10R. High win rate, low earnings.

16
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:49,116
Below 1, the system loses money. Exactly 1, no gain and no loss.

17
00:01:49,565 --> 00:01:56,880
In real markets, over many trades, 1.3 to 2 is often considered good.

18
00:01:57,330 --> 00:02:05,297
But a profit factor of 3 on ten trades tells you nothing. Six wins, four losses, that is all.

19
00:02:06,297 --> 00:02:10,738
A backtest profit factor drops once you add costs.

20
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:17,066
Add 0.1R of spread and commission to every trade of System Y.

21
00:02:17,516 --> 00:02:28,827
Each win becomes 1.9R, each loss 1.1R. The profit factor falls from 1.64 to 1.41.

22
00:02:29,827 --> 00:02:36,018
Profit factor tells you how much you made. It does not tell you how bumpy the ride was.

23
00:02:36,468 --> 00:02:42,109
Sharpe ratio: divide the average return by how much the return swings.

24
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:50,031
The swing is called standard deviation: how far each month's return sits from the average.

25
00:02:51,031 --> 00:02:59,311
System A: an average of 2% a month, with 4% swings. Sharpe 0.5.

26
00:02:59,762 --> 00:03:07,964
System B: an average of 3%, but 9% swings. Sharpe 0.33.

27
00:03:08,414 --> 00:03:15,728
B earns more, but gives more shocks for every percent. A has the cleaner path.

28
00:03:16,179 --> 00:03:29,579
For a yearly Sharpe, multiply the monthly one by the square root of 12, about 3.5. A is about 1.7, B about 1.15.

29
00:03:30,579 --> 00:03:36,769
The real formula also subtracts the risk-free return. We skipped it today to keep it simple.

30
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:45,423
Sharpe also punishes upside jumps. So some people use Sortino, which counts only the downside.

31
00:03:45,873 --> 00:03:57,759
The biggest danger: a martingale-type system shows a great Sharpe for months, until one big shock arrives. Sharpe cannot see what is not in the data.

32
00:03:58,759 --> 00:04:01,684
So build the full report card like this.

33
00:04:02,134 --> 00:04:10,023
Number of trades: at least a hundred. Profit factor after costs. Average R per trade.

34
00:04:10,472 --> 00:04:16,743
The biggest drawdown, and the bad edge from Monte Carlo. And the Sharpe ratio.

35
00:04:17,192 --> 00:04:26,518
And if a backtest shows a profit factor or Sharpe above 3 over many trades, do not celebrate. Get suspicious first.

36
00:04:27,518 --> 00:04:29,086
So, today's summary.

37
00:04:29,536 --> 00:04:36,223
Profit factor is total profit divided by total loss. Win rate alone misleads.

38
00:04:36,673 --> 00:04:41,741
Always check profit factor after costs, and over many trades.

39
00:04:42,191 --> 00:04:50,080
Sharpe is return divided by swings. But it cannot see rare, big shocks.

40
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:56,957
Next episode: overfitting. When a backtest looks better than the truth.

41
00:04:57,407 --> 00:05:02,240
